Numeric map-key normalization oracle/test checkpoint, 2026-10-09. Added four direct pinned Java Fastjson inputs for colliding `Long`/`Integer` keys (`"5"`/`"05"` and `"1"`/`"01"`), with forward and reversed property order. All are accepted and Java retains the value from the last encountered property. The route DTO corpus now has 129 cases; fixture SHA-256 `fd3b3ccb3b9221df48925ab3d20ea36af190705c10b84c9d661275d33a36bf1b`. The focused native `route_dto_inputs_match_invoked_java_fastjson` test fails as expected on the forward broker-address collision: Rust's raw string-key `BTreeMap` ordering retains `"first"` where Java retains `"last"`. No production code changed in this checkpoint; pinned Java source remains read-only. P6-V04 remains PENDING_PHASE_19 and full source/API/input reconciliation remains open.

Numeric map-key normalization parity fix checkpoint, 2026-10-09. Both `Long`→String and `Integer`→Integer map decoders now consume the raw object in property encounter order before coercion and numeric-key insertion. This preserves Java's last-value behavior when distinct textual keys normalize to the same numeric key. The focused 129-case route DTO differential passes after the expected red result. Full `cargo test --workspace --all-targets --locked -q`, strict all-target/all-feature Clippy, pinned offline Maven verify (22 scenarios and deterministic fixture checks), comparator self-test and manifest validation (22), `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, and `git diff --check` pass. Route fixture SHA-256 remains `fd3b3ccb3b9221df48925ab3d20ea36af190705c10b84c9d661275d33a36bf1b`. Pinned Java source remains read-only; Phase 6 reconciliation remains open and P6-V04 stays PENDING_PHASE_19.

